00:00:05.833 --> 00:00:06.750
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: Hi.

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:08.070
I'm Guillaume Laforge.

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:11.090
In this video, my colleague
Ray Tsang and myself

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:14.760
will teach you about Cloud
Functions for Java 11,

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:19.860
how to create a function, test
it locally, and deploy it.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.350
In Cloud Functions
for Java 11, you

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:26.940
can create two types of
functions, HTTP and background

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:27.940
functions.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:30.660
Let's create an HTTP function.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:34.800
It just needs to implement
the HTTP function interface

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.370
and you can get the request
payload and the request object

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:42.250
and write the response to
the response writer object.

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:43.430
And that's it.

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:45.800
To run this function
locally, you

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:51.110
can use the function maven
plugin and run mvn functionrun.

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:54.950
And now the function
will respond on port 8080

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:56.300
on local host.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:00.500
To deploy the function, you can
use the G Cloud command line.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:04.069
Alternatively, you can also
use the function plugin

00:01:04.069 --> 00:01:06.450
and it's deploy go.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:09.800
Now I'll let Ray tell you
about background functions.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:12.230
RAY TSANG: If you want to
respond to events from Cloud

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:16.220
resources like Cloud Storage,
Cloud PubSub, Firestore

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:19.580
and more, then you can
create a background function

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:22.590
to respond to
these Cloud events.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:26.040
Simply implement the
background function interface,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.460
inspect the event object,
and when you deploy,

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:32.820
specify the trigger that you
want to receive the Cloud

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.080
event from.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.330
In addition to our
function framework,

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:39.480
you can also build functions
with your favorite frameworks.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.820
Here we have an example
of a function written

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.250
using Micronaut, using
the Micronaut programming

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:46.050
model and the annotations.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:48.240
And here we have an example
of a function written

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.310
using Spring Cloud Functions.

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:53.730
And the function is just
a Java function annotated

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:56.220
as a spring bean.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:00.570
Last but not least, you can use
any alternative JVM languages,

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:04.660
like Kotlin, Groovy,
Scala, and more.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:08.039
So check out our Cloud Functions
for Java 11 documentation

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:09.339
and give it a try today.

00:02:09.339 --> 00:02:12.389
[MUSIC PLAYING]

